PROCESS_BODY = """<h2>What insurance appraisal is – and isn’t – in Maryland</h2>
<p>Most homeowners and commercial property policies sold in Maryland contain an appraisal clause. It gives you and your insurer a private, structured way to settle one question: how much the covered damage costs to repair or replace. It is not a lawsuit, it is not a complaint to the regulator, and it does not decide whether a loss is covered in the first place. Maryland Insurance Appraiser works only inside that valuation lane, which is why we keep the steps below tightly focused on the amount of loss.</p>
<p>Because appraisal is a creature of contract, the exact rules come from your policy wording. The steps here describe how many common policy forms work, but your declarations page and policy jacket control. For questions about your legal rights, deadlines or coverage, speak with a Maryland attorney.</p>

<h2>The appraisal process, step by step</h2>
<h3>1. Identify the gap</h3>
<p>Appraisal makes sense once the insurer has accepted that a covered loss occurred and has put a number on it that you believe is too low – or, less often, when the insurer thinks your number is too high. Lay the two estimates side by side. Disputes usually concentrate in a handful of places: roof quantities and pitch, whether siding or flooring must be replaced to match, overhead and profit, code-required upgrades, and unit pricing for the Baltimore–Washington market versus rural counties. A <a href="/property-claim-services/carrier-estimate-review/">carrier estimate review</a> can show whether the gap is large enough to justify the time and cost of appraisal.</p>
<h3>2. Make a written demand</h3>
<p>Either party can usually invoke appraisal in writing. Send the demand to the adjuster and any address the policy lists for notices, and keep proof of delivery. State the claim number, date of loss, property address, the amount in dispute and the name of your appraiser if you have chosen one.</p>
<h3>3. Appoint appraisers</h3>
<p>Many policies give each side about 20 days to name a “competent and disinterested” appraiser. Competent means the person understands construction, estimating and pricing; disinterested means they have no personal stake in the result beyond a normal fee.</p>
<h3>4. Choose an umpire</h3>
<p>The two appraisers then try to agree on an umpire. If they cannot, many policy forms allow either party to ask a court to appoint one. A good umpire is someone both sides can trust to read an estimate carefully and to stay out of coverage arguments.</p>
<h3>5. Inspect, measure and negotiate</h3>
<p>The appraisers inspect the property, often together, and exchange estimates, photos and supporting documents. They then negotiate line by line. In practice, most appraisals resolve here without the umpire deciding anything, because two experienced estimators looking at the same roof or kitchen tend to narrow their differences quickly.</p>
<h3>6. Umpire review and award</h3>
<p>Items still in dispute go to the umpire. Once any two of the three sign a written award, it generally fixes the amount of loss. The insurer then applies the deductible, prior payments and any policy limits, and issues payment. Where the policy pays replacement cost, the held-back depreciation is usually paid after repairs are finished.</p>

<h2>How appraisal fits with the Maryland Insurance Administration</h2>
<p>The <strong>Maryland Insurance Administration (MIA)</strong> regulates insurers in the state and takes consumer complaints about claims handling – slow responses, unexplained denials, ignored documents. Maryland’s claims-handling standards appear in COMAR Title 31 and the unfair claim settlement practices law in the Insurance Article. An MIA complaint is a separate track from appraisal. The MIA looks at how a claim was handled; appraisal sets what the damage is worth. Some policyholders pursue both. If you are considering a claim that an insurer failed to act in good faith, Maryland law generally routes that through the MIA first, and you should talk to an attorney before taking that step.</p>

<h2>Who pays for appraisal</h2>
<p>Under most appraisal clauses, each side pays its own appraiser, and the two sides split the umpire’s fees and other shared costs equally. Appraiser fees may be hourly, a flat fee or another arrangement agreed in writing. Ask for the fee structure before you sign anything, and run your own numbers with the <a href="/appraisal-calculators/claim-gap-fee-estimator/">claim gap and fee estimator</a> to see whether the likely recovery outweighs the cost.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Keep the scope narrow</b>Appraisal decides amount, not coverage. If your insurer says the damage isn’t covered at all – for example, it calls a roof leak “wear and tear” or treats water as flood – that coverage question generally belongs with your insurer, the MIA or an attorney, not the appraisal panel.</div>

<h2>What appraisal won’t do</h2>
<ul>
<li>It won’t decide whether a peril is covered or an exclusion applies.</li>
<li>It won’t award bad-faith damages, interest penalties or attorney fees.</li>
<li>It won’t extend the time you have to sue under the policy or Maryland law – check those dates separately.</li>
<li>It won’t produce a quick result if the documentation is thin; missing photos and invoices slow everything down.</li>
<li>It won’t guarantee a higher number. An honest panel can land close to the insurer’s figure if the original estimate was fair.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Documentation checklist</h2>
<p>Before you demand appraisal, gather as much of the following as you can. Our <a href="/appraisal-calculators/appraisal-readiness-checklist/">appraisal readiness checklist</a> walks through each item.</p>
<ol>
<li>The full policy, including the declarations page and every endorsement.</li>
<li>All letters and emails from the insurer, plus the adjuster’s estimate and photos.</li>
<li>Your contractor’s estimate, ideally in a comparable line-item format.</li>
<li>Dated photos and video of the damage before and after any temporary repairs.</li>
<li>Receipts for mitigation work – tarping, water extraction, board-up, tree removal.</li>
<li>Weather records for the date of loss, such as storm reports for your county.</li>
<li>A log of every call and inspection, with names and dates.</li>
</ol>
<p>With those pieces in hand, appraisal becomes a focused conversation about numbers rather than a hunt for paperwork. If you’d like a second opinion on whether your claim is a good fit, you can <a href="/start-a-claim-review/">start a claim review</a> with Maryland Insurance Appraiser.</p>"""

